--- body ---

## Context

Audit finding F4 (`verification/SITTER-LIFECYCLE-AUDIT-2026-09-12.md`).

`manifest.json`'s `update_url` points at
`raw.githubusercontent.com/.../main/plugins/sdt-sitter/update.json`, and ticket
0727 established **with a control** that it is required: the identical build
without it is refused at install ("peut-etre incompatible avec cette version de
Zotero", 0.2.14, 2026-09-06).

So a plugin whose proposition is "local by default" cannot be installed without
the host contacting GitHub about it on the host's own update cadence
(`extensions.update.interval`, 86400 s as read on the author's profile),
carrying the installed version and the user's address. Nothing is wrong with the
code. The disclosure is missing: it is the only network traffic the add-on's
existence causes, and neither `RELEASE-NOTES.md` nor SPEC §5.2.7's privacy
paragraph mentions it.

This matters for the September 21 release, which STATE.md scopes to
correctness, packaging and privacy.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/RELEASE-NOTES.md` — what an installer is told
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.7 — the sitter's privacy paragraph (the one that says the ring
  is volatile and that there is no private durable ledger)
- `SPEC.md` §6 — discloses rather than decides, per AGENTS.md
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/update.json` — its `_comment` already records why
  `update_url` is required; the privacy consequence is not stated there

## Actions

1. State the fact where an installer meets it: the host checks an update
   manifest on GitHub roughly daily for as long as the add-on is installed, the
   check carries the add-on's version and the requesting address, it cannot be
   turned off without making the add-on uninstallable, and nothing about the
   library is sent.
2. Put the same fact once in the owning SPEC section rather than twice in prose
   — AGENTS.md's "one statement per fact", with the other site pointing at it.
3. Do not overstate: the request is Zotero's, not the plugin's; the plugin makes
   no network call of its own. Say that too, since it is the part a reader would
   otherwise assume wrongly.

## Test

- `python3 -m pytest tests/test_sdt_sitter.py -q` stays green — in particular
  `test_manifest_names_no_unresolvable_host` and the UI-string guards, which
  this must not trip.
- If a prose guard (`bench/check_names.py`, `bench/check_figures.py`) has
  anything to say about the new text, satisfy it rather than working around it.

## Verification

- [x] `make check`
- [x] The claim reads as true against `update.json`'s own `_comment` and against
      0727's log entry of 2026-09-06T22:34Z. Quote neither loosely.

## Invariants

- No code change. No design decision: this is disclosure of an existing fact,
  which is what SPEC §6 is for.

## Exit criteria

- An installer can find out, without reading the source, that installing the
  add-on means a daily request to GitHub, and what it carries.
